            <ArticleTitle>Herpes zoster infection as a differential diagnosis of acute pulpitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>143-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Many diseases can cause orofacial pain, and the diagnosis must be established before final treatment. This case report presents a patient with orofacial pain that was diagnosed as an acute pulpitis. However, there was no evidence of this problem on examination. After 4 days, the patient showed multiples vesicles on the face, and a herpes zoster viral infection was diagnosed. The patient was treated with acyclovir and, after 2 yr, she still complains of facial sensitivity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
